# ESP-IDF build output (regenerated by `idf.py build`)
build/
**/build/
# per-variant build dirs from run.ps1 (build_root_wormhole_tree, build_victim_wormhole_tree_B, ...)
build_*/
**/build_*/

# sdkconfig backups / generated headers
sdkconfig.old
**/sdkconfig.old

# CLion / CMake
cmake-build-*/
.idea/

# ── Host-side export output (tools/exports/) ────────────────────────────────
# RAW CAPTURES ARE TRACKED ON PURPOSE. They are the thesis's primary evidence
# and are irreplaceable — the boards get wiped for the next run — so the team
# needs them on GitHub (see commits 69e6057 / 8200955, "for team visibility").
# ~6 MB for the whole current matrix. What we do NOT track:
#
#   _archive/     superseded runs kept only for provenance (~46 MB)
#   trimmed/      derived from raw by trim_run.py; regenerate, don't store
#   manifest.json rewritten by validate_integrity.py on every run
tools/exports/_archive/
tools/exports/**/trimmed/
tools/exports/**/manifest.json

# Stray exports/ folders — NOT real output folders. All four host tools used to
# default their output/scan dir to a bare "exports" resolved against the SHELL'S
# CWD, so running one from the wrong directory created and/or scanned an
# exports/ there. This happened three times for real:
#
#   exports/                 (repo root)   committed in 8200955
#   root_node/exports/       a whole baseline-tree run written here by
#   child_node/exports/      export_logs.py, because that is where you already
#                            stand for `idf.py set-target` / flashing
#
# export_logs.py was the damaging one — it WRITES captures, so irreplaceable
# data landed somewhere no analysis command looks. All four defaults now
# resolve next to their own script, so this should never recur; these rules
# stay as a backstop. Real captures live in tools/exports/.
/exports/
/root_node/exports/
/child_node/exports/

# Host-side analysis output (regenerated by run.ps1 -Analyze / the M6-M8 scripts).
#
# feature_table.csv and eda_output/ ARE tracked (2026-07-27). They are derived and
# regenerable, but they are also the deliverable a reader is most likely to want to
# LOOK at — the 16-feature table and the EDA plots — and requiring a clone plus a
# pipeline run to see a correlation heatmap is a poor trade for ~17 MB. Tracking them
# also removes an inconsistency: baseline/linear and blackhole/linear had been tracked
# since before this rule existed, so GitHub showed analysis for two datasets and not
# the other seven, which looked like missing work rather than a deliberate exclusion.
#
# windowed_dataset.csv stays ignored: it is a pure intermediate between preprocess.py
# and features.py, nothing reads it directly, and it is the larger of the two.
analysis/**/windowed_dataset.csv

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# OS junk
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store


how to run esp32.txt
